If $n\in N$, prove that $2n+ 3n$ is a multiple or odd multiples of 5 if n is a multiple of 5 (.gcoutka to dieaja).
Because multiplicative function, adding numbers is distributive, you can easily see that $2n+3n$ is equal to $5n$.  $N$ is a multiple of 5 if and only if it is a multiple of 5 and vice versa, because 5 is a prime.  Therefore, $5n$ is a multiple of 5 if and only if it is a multiple of 5 .

Now we prove it by induction.  For $n=1$, $5\cdot1=5$ is a multiple of 5, so $P$ is true for $n=1$.  Assume $P$ is true for $n$ and prove that it is true for $n+1$ as well; that is, we must prove that the right side is even if $n$ is even and that it is odd if $n$ is odd.  If $n$ is even, $5\cdot(n+1)=5\cdot n+5\cdot1=5\cdot n+ 5$.  This expression is even because $5\cdot n$ is even when $n$ is even (distributive).  If $n$ is odd, $5\cdot(n+1)=5\cdot n+51=5\cdot n+ 5$.  This expression is odd because $5\cdot n$ is odd even $n$ is even (distributive).

Since you proved it in both cases it holds $\forall n\in N$.

